mitigation:
Validate the redirect target by parsing it as a URL and comparing origins, instead of pattern-matching the raw string. In `safeNext()`:

    function safeNext() {
      var raw = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('next') || '/';
      // reject control chars / whitespace outright
      if (/[\u0000-\u0020\u007f]/.test(raw)) return '/';
      var u;
      try { u = new URL(raw, location.origin); } catch (e) { return '/'; }
      if (u.origin !== location.origin) return '/';   // catches //evil.com, /\evil.com, /\t/evil.com
      return u.pathname + u.search + u.hash;          // re-serialised, never the raw input
    }

The URL parser strips tabs/newlines, so `/\t/evil.com` normalises to `//evil.com` and is caught by the origin comparison; backslashes are normalised to `/` for special schemes, so `/\evil.com` is caught too. Re-serialising from the parsed URL also guarantees the value handed to `location.replace()` cannot contain characters the URL parser would re-interpret.

Defence in depth: keep the post-login redirect server-side — accept `next` on `POST /auth/login`, resolve it server-side against the gateway's own origin, reject anything that is not a same-origin path, and 302 to the validated path (the nginx-generated redirect at backend/app.py:7361/7365 already injects an unencoded `$request_uri` into `next`, so server-side validation there is the more reliable place). As a minimum, add `if (/[\u0000-\u0020\u007f]/.test(raw)) return '/';` to the existing checks.

description:
MECHANISM (verified line by line; no request was executed).

Entry: POST /api/ddns/save (backend/app.py:6314), body {services:[...]}.

Validation gap: _validate_ddns_services (backend/app.py:6226) allow-lists exactly ONE field - ipv6_source, which must be 'router' or match rule_\d+ (backend/app.py:6245-6248). Every other field of every service object is written into the UCI ddns package verbatim:
  service_name   backend/app.py:6398  - no allow-list against DDNS_PROVIDER_DIR (that directory is only ever listed for the UI at backend/app.py:6192-6204, never enforced on write);
  domain / lookup_host  backend/app.py:6414-6415 - built from the raw main_domain / sub_domain;
  username backend/app.py:6420, password backend/app.py:6437, interface backend/app.py:6438;
  ip_source backend/app.py:6471/6479/6484, ip_network backend/app.py:6486;
  param_enc backend/app.py:6488, param_opt backend/app.py:6489;
  check_interval backend/app.py:6491-6492 - str(check_interval) with NO numeric check at all.

The only filtering applied is inside _uci_set (backend/app.py:6142-6171): str(value).replace('\n',' ').replace('\r',' ') at backend/app.py:6169. Quotes, backticks, command substitution, semicolons, pipes and backslashes survive into the UCI store. The uci invocation itself is list-form argv (backend/app.py:6170), so there is NO shell injection at the uci call itself - the concern is what the CONSUMER does with the stored value.

Sink: OpenWrt's ddns-scripts package (installed by setup/setup.sh:1404-1407) reads the ddns UCI package and evaluates parts of it as root. The in-repo evidence for that eval semantics is explicit but narrow: the developers state that ip_script is 'executed by ddns-scripts as a ROOT eval' (backend/app.py:6233-6234 and 6446) and record an empirically confirmed exploit on device .233 at backend/app.py:6448-6454. The same root-eval consumption of the OTHER section options (service_name selects the provider script, param_opt is appended to the provider's update parameters) is NOT provable from this repository: ddns-scripts is an external OpenWrt package and is not vendored here, and network access was not permitted for this verification. That is exactly why this entry is UNCERTAIN rather than CONFIRMED - the injection up to the UCI store is confirmed, the root-eval sink is not.

The UCI section name itself is NOT injectable: uci_name is sanitised with re.sub(r'[^a-zA-Z0-9_]', '_', svc_name) (backend/app.py:6394).

REQUIRES AN EXISTING PANEL SESSION: /api/ddns/save is not exempt in before_request (backend/app.py:842-847). The panel already runs as root, so this is a defence-in-depth / blast-radius issue rather than a privilege-boundary crossing - MEDIUM, not CRITICAL. The baseline rated the sibling ipv6_source issue CRITICAL with privileges_required NONE; that rating is NOT supported by the current source, where the endpoint has required a panel session since commit 5aa8ed6 (2026-09-13, 'v0.7.9: 安全加固'), which introduced the global _require_auth interceptor (backend/app.py:795-872) - well before the 2026-10-29 baseline.
